Wiring contracts stay at function parameters. They cannot be stored in locals,
struct fields, choice payloads, collections, aliases, defaults or return slots.
An ordinary message remains a choice value.

Templates also remain ordinary String values. Composition, slots, wrappers,
formatting and fragment insertion do not attach hidden Wire identity, Route
application, subscriptions or rerender work.

Function bodies, branches and loops use statement blocks. A colon opens each
block and a semicolon closes it rather than ending an individual statement.
Statements run in source order until control flow exits or redirects
the block, and each nested block creates a child scope. Moth has no general bare
block for grouping statements.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    greet |name String| -> String:
        if name is "Priya":
            return [: Hello, [name]!]
        ;

        return [: Hello, [name].]
    ;
]

The first semicolon closes the `if` block and the last closes the function.
Inside each `[: ...]` template, square brackets build a String and the colon
separates the template head from its body. That colon does not open a statement
block. See the @../language-overview/#blocks-and-statements (Blocks and
statements) reference and @../templates/#head-and-body (Template head and body).

### Channel construction

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    reader, writer Channel of AppMessage = Channel()
]

The annotation supplies the carried type. `reader` and `writer` are
directional endpoints of one channel. Channel transport, scheduling and
cancellation are separate from Wiring. See @#wiring-introduction (Wiring reference) for details.
